## Why This Is Asked

Tests understanding of MVCC, anomalies, and cross-batch correctness in a high-concurrency payout workflow.

## Key Concepts
- MVCC, SERIALIZABLE, SSI
- Row-level locking, SELECT FOR UPDATE
- Idempotent writes, upserts, optimistic locking, version/timestamp columns
- Long-running transactions and abort rates

## Code Example
```sql
BEGIN;
SET TRANSACTION ISOLATION LEVEL SERIALIZABLE;
SELECT balance, version FROM drivers WHERE id = :driver_id FOR UPDATE;
-- compute new_balance using :amount and existing balance/version
UPDATE drivers SET balance = new_balance, version = version + 1 WHERE id = :driver_id;
INSERT INTO payout_ledger(driver_id, amount, tstamp) VALUES (:driver_id, :amount, NOW());
COMMIT;
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test for write skew in this setup?
- When would you introduce compensating transactions if an abort occurs?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of ACID in MVCC, practical use of SERIALIZABLE, and safe concurrent transfers with FOR UPDATE and retry logic.

## Key Concepts
- ACID, MVCC, SERIALIZABLE
- SELECT ... FOR UPDATE
- Serialization failure (SQLSTATE 40001) and retry
- Edge cases: insufficient funds, same-account transfers

## Code Example
```sql
-- pseudo-sql for clarity
BEGIN;
SELECT balance INTO from_bal FROM accounts WHERE id = :from FOR UPDATE;
SELECT balance INTO to_bal FROM accounts WHERE id = :to FOR UPDATE;
IF from_bal >= :amount THEN
  UPDATE accounts SET balance = from_bal - :amount WHERE id = :from;
  UPDATE accounts SET balance = to_bal + :amount WHERE id = :to;
  COMMIT;
ELSE
  ROLLBACK;
END IF;
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you implement automatic retries with backoff on serialization failures?
- How would you test concurrency scenarios to ensure no overdrafts occur?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of cross-store writes with explicit TTLs and cache invalidation. It checks how writes propagate across Redis, MongoDB, Cassandra, and DynamoDB, plus how to keep a hot cache coherent.

## Key Concepts
- Cross-store write paths with idempotence
- TTL-based bucketing in Cassandra
- Cache invalidation and refresh strategies in Redis
- Read path with locality and fallback

## Code Example
```javascript
async function writeMessage(chatId, userId, text, ts){
  await redis.hset(`chat:presence:${userId}`, 'online', 1);
  const msgId = generateId();
  await mongo.messages.insertOne({chat_id: chatId, msg_id: msgId, sender_id: userId, ts, text});
  await cassandra.execute("INSERT INTO messages_by_chat (chat_id, bucket, ts, msg_id) VALUES (?, bucket(?), ?, ?)", [chatId, ts, ts, msgId]);
  await dynamo.archive({chat_id: chatId, msg_id: msgId, ts, text});
  await redis.publish(`chat:updates:${chatId}`, JSON.stringify({msg_id: msgId}));
}
```
